 1 big thing: GOP&#x27;s brutal triage 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Republican super PACs are flooding the airwaves in once-safe states and are pulling down ads for races that look like lost causes, Axios' Hans Nichols writes. 

 Why it matters: GOP operatives are making the kind of hard decisions of a battlefield medic — save the wounded, and say a prayer for the lost. Between the lines: Republicans can dismiss bad polls as noise, or the result of liberal bias. But their concern about a potential blue wave is evident in their thundering attack ads in Kansas, South Carolina and Texas.

 GOP efforts now are more focused on turning out MAGA loyalists than on persuading swing voters. President Trump shakes hands with Republican Senate candidate Ken Paxton at a rally in San Antonio yesterday. Photo: Win McNamee/Getty Images 👢 Republicans have been forced to pour well over $100 million into Ken Paxton's Senate race in Texas against Democrat James Talarico, and to plead for millions more to defend Senate seats in Alaska, Iowa and Ohio. 

 President Trump held a rally in San Antonio yesterday to try to boost Paxton — the president's second trip in less than a week to Texas, a state he won by 14 points in 2024. 🌊 During the past week, the president has also campaigned in Oklahoma, Alabama, Ohio and Nebraska. He'll fly to Alabama again on Saturday for the Georgia-Alabama football game. 

