 2. 🦊 Inside Trump&#x27;s fight with Fox 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Alex Wroblewski/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Republicans have spent months griping privately to Fox News about coverage they argue is helping Abdul El-Sayed, Michigan's Democratic nominee for Senate, moderate his image, Axios' Alex Isenstadt writes. 

 The frustration boiled over into public view Tuesday when President Trump lashed out at his favorite network. Why it matters: Republicans worry that Fox is undercutting their efforts to brand the charismatic El-Sayed as a liberal who's far outside the mainstream.

 Polls show El-Sayed, a critic of Israel's government who'd be the first Muslim elected to the U.S. Senate, in a tight race against former Rep. Mike Rogers (R). Behind the scenes: White House officials reached out to Fox on Tuesday to complain about anchor Martha MacCallum's interview with El-Sayed, according to people familiar with the conversations. On Truth Social, Trump blasted the interview as "softball."

 Senior GOP operatives repeatedly contacted Fox producers and executives in early August, warning that El-Sayed could use favorable commentary from host Jesse Watters to cast himself as moderate. 📺 Later that month, El-Sayed released a TV ad featuring Watters' remark that "You gotta respect Abdul because he beat the [Democratic] machine."

 A Fox News spokesperson said Rogers also was invited to be interviewed on Watters' show but didn't respond. Share this story . 

 
 
