 3. 🎂 &quot;The Axios Show&quot;: Making 300 the new 70 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios' Madison Mills interviews Bryan Johnson at his Los Angeles home on Monday. Photo: Daniel Hollis Diamond for Axios Bryan Johnson, 49, the face of the longevity movement, says on a new episode of " The Axios Show ," out this morning, that he wants to make staying alive more important than making money.

 The famous/infamous biohacker spends over $2 million a year on his health, talks about living forever and publicly tracks everything from his erections to his sperm strength. Why it matters: As Johnson fights to "live forever," he's doing so with a wink to his 8 million social media followers, a link to his business in his bio and a knack for drawing attention to himself.

 In his interview with Axios' Madison Mills, Johnson offered advice for Elon Musk and said he'd love to help President Trump with his health.

 "Will the 25th century ... be like, 'thank you' in 500 years? Will they give me a head nod? That's the goal," he said. His prescription: Change the focus of society to health, not wealth.

 "Society says making money ... is the greatest trophy one can get ... We are at this inflection moment as a species where we are transitioning to 'Don't Die,'" Johnson said, referring to the title of his longevity movement. "What creates status and power and prestige is going to be different. And as crazy as it sounds, it may be your nighttime erections ... It may be your ability to dunk a basketball at age 49." ▶️ Watch the full episode . 

 
 
