 8. 🐢 1 fun thing: Tortoise aging secrets 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Jonathan, an Aldabra giant tortoise who lives 1,200 miles off Africa's west coast on the remote island of St. Helena. Photo: Teeny Lucy via The New York Times The world's oldest known land animal — Jonathan, a roughly 194-year-old giant tortoise — is giving scientists a new window into aging, the N.Y. Times writes. 

 Researchers sequenced his genome for the first time and compared it with those of tortoises 100 years younger, finding differences in genes involved in DNA repair and protecting chromosomes. The 400-pound tortoise, who lives on the remote South Atlantic island of St. Helena, is mostly blind but still has excellent hearing and enjoys greeting visitors and having his neck petted.

 But getting his DNA wasn't easy: The first attempt to swab his cheek yielded nothing but half-chewed carrot. Go deeper (gift link). 

 
 
